Finally, even democratic decision-making requires some entrenchment
against the democratic will. For example, democratic parliaments would find
it highly desirable to have a rule — an entrenched one — saying that those who
lose a legislative battle over the enactment of a law may not reintroduce the
issue immediately. Indeed, it might be desirable to have an entrenched rule
against reintroducing the issue during the same legislative term, perhaps with
an exception when reintroduction is favored by a supermajority. Those rules,
however, differ in degree but not in kind from the constitutionally entrenched
rules that Waldron attacks.
